  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate Resilient by Nature (CRxN), implemented by WWF-Australia, is using equitable nature-based solutions (NbS) to make Pacific and Southeast Asian communities more socially, economically, and ecologically resilient. A total of 10,355 individuals across 150 communities have directly participated in NbS projects with over 42,000 hectares under conservation or restoration and 152,382 hectares benefiting from better management practices. 
&#x200d;
Project results reflect the diverse relationships between communities, landscapes and livelihoods in the Indo-Pacific region. In the Pacific, the creation of marine protected areas, managed mangrove  protection and agroforestry activities have led to increased numbers of fish and sharks as well as improved biodiversity, soil health and productivity. In the Mekong, nature-positive production in wetlands, like floating rice farming, has enhanced sedimentation, water retention, and fish catch rates.
&#x200d;
To support partner governments, businesses and other implementing partners strengthen NbS approaches, CRxN continues to support partnerships and has produced 105 knowledge products and conducted 88 learning events since the start of the program. In Vanuatu, CRxN hosted a national workshop on carbon-funded invasive species management and forest regeneration. The workshop generated support for pilot activities and consideration of an invasive species removal national initiative.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Led by Kyeema Foundation Australia in partnership with WWF, the Community-led coral reef restoration initiative supports young indigenous scientists and community leaders from Pacific Island Countries to restore compromised reef ecosystems and maintain the protective climate-related services they provide. The initiative does this by identifying, securing, and propagating bleaching resistant &#x2018;super corals&#x2019; in select coastal sites, with the goal of preventing local coral species extinction in the face of climate change. 
&#x200d;
In partnership with Corals4Conservation (C4C), the initiative has supported 19 young indigenous scientists from Fiji, PNG, and Samoa to receive training on methods for identifying and testing the heat tolerance of select corals. 6 scientific test nurseries have also been established, in Fiji, PNG and Samoa to propagate resilient &#x2018;super-corals&#x2019; which can be used to develop coral reef restoration sites.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Natural ecosystems in Timor Leste (Baucau and Viqueque) have been depleted through intensive deforestation, slash-and-burn agriculture and heavy rains, resulting in degraded soil, landslides and degraded ecosystems. Through the Natured-Based Solutions Initiative, WWF and Caritas utilise agroforestry as a nature-based solution to address these challenges. Community members are repopulating their household plots and rehabilitating degraded forests. Tree species include fast growing varieties that can be utilised for firewood and fencing and species that offer &#x201c;protection&#x201d; by improving soil health and preventing landslides. So far, 73,000 saplings have been distributed to support the implementation of community forest plans and a total of 406 hectares of land are under restoration.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">WWF in Solomon Islands expanded their sustainable coastal community programs in Western Province, working with four communities: Pusiju, Saeraghi, Boboe, and Kongulavata on nature-based solutions (NbS). 
&#x200d;
The NbS focuses on sustainable management of sea-grapes, a high-value and nutritious algae that incentivises protection and restoration of climate-critical coastal ecosystems. Overall, the project has strengthened planning and capacity for community-level natural resource management, including through developing Fisheries Management Plans and protecting mangrove ecosystems. Community engagement has led to improved marine resource management and the establishment of Management Committees. Three rounds of sea grape cover monitoring shows significant positive trends. 
&#x200d;
Efforts to foster a communal mindset and ownership of natural wealth are ongoing, with a focus on livelihood impacts, particularly for women. Business models for nature-positive ventures focused on sea grape products, are being explored through launching and testing products in the domestic market.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
